  // ── DevTools Mini-Quest ──
  'tools.devtoolsMini.title': 'The Browser Detective — Your DevTools Mini-Quest',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.intro': 'The browser developer tools (DevTools) are the secret of professional web developers. With F12 you open a control center that reveals all the secrets of a website. In 5 minutes you will learn the most important features.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step1.title': 'Step 1: Open the Secret Door',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step1.p1': 'Open your own website (or any page) in the browser and press <strong>F12</strong> (Windows) or <strong>Cmd + Option + I</strong> (Mac). A panel opens — that\'s the DevTools. Click the <strong>Elements</strong> symbol at the top (usually the first icon on the left).',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step1.p2': '<strong>What you see:</strong> On the left the HTML tree of your page, on the right the corresponding CSS rules. That is the DNA of your website — everything you wrote in VS Code is visible here.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step2.title': 'Step 2: Live Editing',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step2.p1': 'Click on an <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> in the Elements panel. You can edit the text directly in the panel — without opening VS Code! Change the text and press Enter. The browser immediately shows the new result.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step2.p2': '<strong>Aha moment:</strong> This is no trick — this is how pros test their changes before saving the code. If you don\'t like a color, you change it live here and then copy only the final value back to VS Code.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step3.title': 'Step 3: CSS Experimenting',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step3.p1': 'Select an element and look at the right panel. There you see all CSS rules that apply to this element. Click on a color (e.g. <code>color: #E67E22</code>) — a color picker appears! Try different colors without saving anything.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step3.p2': 'Add a new CSS property: Click in the empty field below the existing rules and type <code>background-color: yellow;</code>. The element immediately glows yellow — but only in your browser. The original file remains unchanged.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step3.p3': '<strong>Why this is genius:</strong> You can test 20 different color combinations in 30 seconds. Without saving, without reloading. This is the fastest feedback loop in web development.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step4.title': 'Step 4: Simulate the Phone',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step4.p1': 'Click the <strong>device icon</strong> at the top left of the DevTools (second icon from the left, looks like a phone in front of a tablet). The page immediately changes to a mobile view. You can open the device selection at the top and switch between iPhone, iPad, Samsung Galaxy and many others.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step4.p2': '<strong>Pro tip:</strong> Drag the right edge of the phone frame back and forth. This way you test all screen widths between phone and desktop — the fastest way to check responsive layouts.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step5.title': 'Step 5: The Console',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step5.p1': 'Click the <strong>Console</strong> tab at the top of the DevTools (sometimes labeled "Console"). Here you see error messages, warnings, and can enter JavaScript commands yourself. Type:',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step5.p2': 'Press Enter. You see the output immediately. The console is the window to the soul of your website — here you learn what happens in the background, even when nothing is visible.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.step5.p3': '<strong>Remember:</strong> If your page doesn\'t work, open the console FIRST. 90% of all errors show up here as red text with a line number. It is like the error code of your car — without it you are just guessing.',
  'tools.devtoolsMini.cheatTitle': 'Your DevTools Cheat Sheet',
